  "description": "Instructions hidden with a zero font size or a zero opacity, which makes text invisible to humans but readable by agents. GLS-HI-002 lists font-size:0 and opacity:0 as words, but the text is folded before words are looked up and the fold turns every 0 into an o, so those two words could never be found there. This rule reads them as a complete zero declaration instead (the property, a colon, the number 0, an optional unit, then the end of the declaration), and only when the declaration starts at the start of the value or after a semicolon, outside every comment, quoted string and bracket, and no later declaration of the same property in that value replaces it. The value must belong to the first attribute of the tag named exactly style (not data-style, not a value quoted inside another attribute), blanks being the HTML ones (space, tab, line feed, form feed, carriage return), and the text of that same element, up to the next tag, must carry an instruction aimed at the reader or the agent: an order to ignore or reveal the rules or the system prompt, a statement to the user that the work is complete or fine, an order not to mention it, an agent addressed with a modal verb and a command, or a claim that the reader is an AI. The text is read, not parsed: a tag written inside an HTML comment, a script or a textarea is read as a tag. A stylesheet rule is never read, so ordinary zero states (a fade class, a reset, an animation) stay clean.",
